  "narrative_body": "Romania's governing coalition stands three days from a self-imposed reckoning, and the party threatening to blow it up has fewer options than its public posture suggests.\n\nThe Social Democrats (PSD) have called an internal party consultation for April 20 — billed as a \"Moment of Truth\" — in which roughly 5,000 members including parliamentarians, county leaders, and local officials will vote on whether to pull the party from government. PSD says the coalition \"in its current form cannot continue,\" and the party has threatened mass ministerial resignations if Ilie Bolojan, the prime minister, does not step down before the April 23 cabinet meeting. Mr Bolojan has publicly refused, citing constitutional legitimacy, and constitutional experts back him: even if PSD exits, he may continue as caretaker prime minister for up to two years without a parliamentary confidence vote. Nicușor Dan, the president, has blocked the only obvious alternative: he has flatly refused to appoint a government backed by the far-right Alliance for the Union of Romanians (AUR) with Călin Georgescu as prime minister. George Simion, AUR's leader, confirmed Georgescu as a prime-minister candidate this week by meeting with him — but Mr Dan's veto closes that path. PSD and AUR also lack the parliamentary votes to pass a censure motion without the Save Romania Union (USR) or the Democratic Alliance of Hungarians in Romania (UDMR), neither of which will cooperate. Cătălin Predoiu, the National Liberal Party (PNL) deputy prime minister and interior minister, kept to his normal schedule this week — attending official ceremonies on April 15 — signalling that PNL, USR, and UDMR ministers are not preparing to follow PSD out. The caretaker path is open. Whether PSD's consultation amounts to a genuine rupture or an elaborate bid to extract concessions without actually leaving is the question before April 20.\n\nRussian military intelligence hackers — the Fancy Bear group, also known as APT28 — compromised at least 67 email accounts belonging to Romanian Air Force personnel, including at NATO-integrated airbases. Reuters investigated the breach, which is part of a broader intelligence collection campaign across NATO's southeastern flank. Romania's defence ministry said the breach had been contained, a claim that cannot be verified from open sources. The context matters: the Romanian Intelligence Service (SRI) has been without a permanent civilian director for more than two and a half years. A confirmed penetration of military email systems at NATO facilities during this gap is more than a security incident — it compounds an existing vulnerability. A competing reading holds that Fancy Bear's campaign spans multiple southeastern NATO members, suggesting routine signals collection against the alliance rather than an escalation targeting Romania specifically. The institutional context is no less troubling for that.\n\nFiscal pressures continue on a slower clock. The Ministry of Energy confirmed a €150m tranche for energy storage batteries under Romania's EU recovery fund programme, and Mr Dan received Dragoș Pîslaru, the minister of investments and European projects, at the presidential palace — suggesting the president is personally watching EU fund disbursement. Romania must hit major milestones by August 2026 or risk losing up to €10bn in recovery funds; disbursement stood at roughly 37.6% as of October 2025. Individual tranches help at the margins but leave the structural gap intact. A government that enters extended caretaker status before August 2026 would put that deadline under real pressure — which means the April 20 coalition vote carries fiscal stakes well beyond domestic politics.",
  "other_stories": [
